     "text": [
      "You *can* physically do a tattoo or piercing yourself, but it’s strongly discouraged—especially for anything beyond a very superficial, temporary mark.\n",
      "\n",
      "- **Tattooing yourself:** Risks include serious infection, blood-borne disease, allergic reactions, scarring/keloids, nerve or tissue damage, and a permanently uneven result. DIY equipment may not be sterile, and household disinfectants don’t reliably make it safe.\n",
      "- **Piercing yourself:** Risks include infection, abscesses, excessive bleeding, nerve damage, embedded jewelry, scarring, and cartilage damage. Cartilage piercings can develop serious infections that require medical treatment.\n",
      "\n",
      "The safer option is a **licensed, reputable professional** who uses sealed, single-use needles, an autoclave for reusable equipment, gloves, and appropriate aftercare instructions. Avoid piercing the **eyelids, tongue, genitals, nipples, or cartilage** yourself entirely.\n",
      "\n",
      "If you already did it, seek medical care promptly for spreading redness, increasing pain or swelling, pus, fever, red streaks, numbness, or bleeding that won’t stop. Make sure your **tetanus vaccination** is up to date. If you’re under 18, local consent laws may also apply.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
